# AGENTS.md

A CAITS project (Clean Architecture In TypeScript): a Marko app with a backend and a frontend. A harness checks every change.

## Commands

- `npm run check`: all the checks at once: types, lint, architecture rules, unit tests (100% coverage), mutation tests (100% score), end-to-end tests. The work is done only when it passes. It prints only the output of the checks that fail.
- `npm run tdd`: the unit tests, then at each save the tests of the changed files. In an AI agent, Vitest runs them once and stops: run it again after a change.
- `npm run lint:architecture`: the architecture rules alone, fast.
- `npx caits mutation-summary --survivors`: the mutants that survived the last mutation run.

## Where the code goes

- The business lives in bounded contexts: `src/backend/<context>/` and `src/frontend/<context>/`, each with `domain/`, `infra/`, its use cases in `command/` and `query/`, and a `di.ts`. A frontend context has `event/` and `presentation/` too. To add one, copy the shape of an existing one.
- Domain: entities, value objects, errors, domain events, and ports (`domain/port/<port>/`), each with its contract (`<Port>.contract.ts`).
- Use cases: one folder per use case, `command/<use-case>/` or `query/<use-case>/`, which holds all of it: its DTOs, its handler and its test, `<use-case>.application.test.ts`, and in the backend its controllers, `<use-case>.<name>.controller.ts` (`<use-case>.http.controller.ts` for HTTP), each with its test, `<use-case>.<name>.presentation.test.ts`. An endpoint lives in one folder, and the name of a test says the layer that it tests. A use case imports nothing from another use case: what they share lives in the domain.
- Infra: the adapters of the ports, in `infra/<port>/`. The test of each adapter runs the contract of its port.
- Frontend: its events, one file each in `event/`, and its presentation, which serves several use cases: the views (`presentation/view/<view>/`), which the routes place; the components, `presentation/component/<component>/`, the parts that the views place; and the store (`presentation/store/`), if the context has use cases. A view and a component take the frontend of their context only, publish its events and read its store. A view imports no other view, and a component no view.
- The contexts talk through the context shared, `src/backend/shared/` and `src/frontend/shared/`, and their context map, `context-map.ts` beside their di.ts. A handler (in the frontend, also a view or a component) publishes an event of its context; the context map, `share(Event, event => [...])`, turns it into events of shared on the shared bus (the event of the context holds what they need). The context map of each context that hears it, `when(Event, event => [...])`, turns it into input DTOs of the context (backend) or into events of the context (frontend). Each line goes one way, reads only its event, and the lines make no cycle. A context names only itself and shared, never another context: only its context map imports the events of shared.
- A component of the frontend context shared is a Marko tag, `component/<name>/index.marko`: a context places it by its name (`<badge>`), and `src/frontend/marko.json` gives Marko their folder.
- Dependencies point to the domain. A context imports its own files with `#context/`, and the context shared of its side with `#shared/` (backend) or `#frontend-shared/` (frontend), never with `../`.
- `src/routes/` holds the pages and the HTTP handlers, and no logic and no test: a page places views, a handler gives the request to a controller. A document that the pages give in another format than HTML (a sitemap, a feed) has its route in a folder named after its file, `` `sitemap.xml`/+handler.ts ``, which gives the GET of a view. The code that a test reads lives in a context.

## Rules

- Write the test first. The coverage and the mutation score stay at 100%.
- A test reads only its own context and shared: no test crosses contexts.
- A check that fails names the file and the rule: fix the code, not the rule.
- No comment turns off a check (`eslint-disable`, `@ts-ignore`, `v8 ignore`, `Stryker disable`), and no test is skipped. An exception lives in `quality/`, as narrow as it can be.
- Do not change `quality/` (the rules, the thresholds, the configuration of the tools) without asking.

